  "pre": "# Venn Diagram — Class Notes\n\n## BEFORE THE CLASS\n\n**What this topic is about**\nOverlapping groups where the overlaps *are* (at least partly) given — people reading several newspapers, students playing several sports, firms selecting candidates. You draw regions, fill them in, and answer counting questions. The hard parts are drawing four sets, reading the word \"only\" correctly, and answering \"maximum possible / minimum possible\" questions.\n\n**How the teaching goes**\nIt starts by building a four-set diagram from scratch — expect a lot of time on the drawing itself. Then a long sports set (four sports, 460 students) is used to teach \"maximum but minimum\" distribution. After that come shorter sets: consulting firms, newspapers with an unknown region, a magazines set based on multiples of numbers, an events set where groups only overlap their neighbours, and a job-fair set that's pure max/min.\n\n**Things to watch for, in the order they'll come up**\n\n1. **The four-set drawing is not four circles.** He draws a 3×3 grid and four overlapping loops across it. Watch which cell is \"all four\" (the centre), which four cells are \"exactly three\" (the ring around the centre), which six are \"exactly two\", which four are \"only one\". He'll count them — 4C3 = 4, 4C2 = 6 — to prove nothing's missing. Draw it yourself while he does.\n\n2. **The word \"only\".** \"20 play only TT and badminton\" fills one cell. \"People playing TT and badminton\" is that cell *plus* everything inside both loops. He'll come back to this in the consulting-firms set, where \"each pair selected 50\" *without* \"only\" means the pairwise cell is 50 minus the threes minus the four. Most wrong answers in this topic are this.\n\n3. **Filling order.** He always starts at the deepest intersection (all four), then threes, then twos, then singles, then checks each circle's total, then the grand total. Note that \"grand total\" means people who do at least one — subtract the \"none\" group first.\n\n4. **Circle totals with a letter in them.** \"Cricket = A + 163\" where A is the only-cricket cell. He adds the whole diagram, sets it equal to the number of people doing at least one, and gets A + B + C + D = 123. That leftover number is what gets \"distributed\" in the next questions.\n\n5. **\"Maximum but minimum\".** \"TT has more players than any other sport; find the least TT can be.\" He explains it with chocolates: bring everyone level, hand out leftovers one at a time, give the last one to TT so it's just ahead. Then the reverse: \"badminton has the fewest; find the most it can be.\" Same equalising, but he backs off one step when the ordering would break. This stretch is long and confusing the first time — that's normal.\n\n6. **Unknown region = a variable, then inequalities.** In the newspaper set every region ends up as something like 18 − a or a − 10. He says each must be ≥ 0, collects the bounds, and often the total comes out as (number ± 2a), so only even (or only odd) answers are possible. Watch him eliminate options by parity.\n\n7. **Two unusual diagrams.** Multiples of 3, 4, 7, 15 → multiples of 15 sit *inside* multiples of 3, and each region = 365 ÷ LCM. Events where group n only overlaps n−1 and n+1 → circles in a chain, not a four-set diagram. The point of both is \"don't force the standard picture\".\n\n**Where people usually get lost**\n- Treating \"A and B\" as \"only A and B\".\n- Starting from the outside of the diagram instead of the centre.\n- In max/min questions, giving all the leftovers to one group and calling it a day.",
  "post": "# Venn Diagram — Class Notes\n\n## REVISION SHEET\n\n**Drawing**\n- 2 or 3 sets: circles.\n- 4 sets: 3×3 grid, four loops. Centre cell = all four. Four cells around it = exactly three. Six remaining inner cells = exactly two. Four outer cells = only one. (Check: 1 + 4 + 6 + 4 = 15 regions.)\n- Sets defined by multiples (every 3rd day, every 15th day): a set nested inside another if one period divides the other. Each region = total ÷ LCM of the periods of the loops it sits in, minus the deeper regions already counted.\n- Groups that only overlap their neighbours (1 with 2, 2 with 3, 3 with 4): circles in a chain. Non-adjacent overlaps are zero.\n\n**Word decoder**\n| Phrase | Region(s) |\n|---|---|\n| \"only A and B\" | the single A∩B cell |\n| \"A and B\" / \"both A and B\" | that cell + every three and the four that contain A and B |\n| \"each pair selected 50\" (no \"only\") | 2s + 3s + 4 = 50 for that pair |\n| \"at most one\" | none + only-one cells |\n| \"at least three\" | threes + four |\n| \"exactly two\" | the six 2-cells only |\n| \"not X\" | everything outside X's loop |\n\n**Filling order**\n1. Deepest region first (all sets), then threes, twos, singles.\n2. Each outer region = circle total − everything already inside that circle.\n3. Grand total of the diagram = people in at least one set = total − \"none\".\n4. If circle totals contain a letter (e.g. cricket = A + 163), add the whole diagram, equate to the at-least-one total, and you get the sum of the unknowns (e.g. A + B + C + D = 123).\n\n**Unknown region method**\n1. Put *a* in the unknown region. Express every other region using *a*.\n2. Every region ≥ 0 → collect bounds. Take the largest lower bound and the smallest upper bound.\n3. Grand total is usually (number ± 2a) → the answer has fixed parity; strike options of the wrong parity.\n4. Substitute the extreme values of *a* for min and max of whatever's asked.\n\n**Max / min distribution (chocolate method inside a Venn)**\n| Wording | Meaning | Steps |\n|---|---|---|\n| \"X is the largest; find the least X can be\" | maximum-but-minimum | Bring every group up to the current largest. Split the remaining leftover equally. Give the last unit(s) to X so it's strictly ahead by 1 |\n| \"X is the smallest; find the most X can be\" | minimum-but-maximum | Bring every group level. Split leftovers equally *but* stop one step short so each other group stays ≥ X + 1. If handing X one more would force the others to need more than what's left, don't |\n| \"A > B > C, minimise A\" | A just ahead | Split as evenly as possible with A getting the extra: 97 → 33, 32, 32 |\n| \"A > B > C, maximise C\" | all three as close as possible | Split nearly equally but strictly ordered: 35 → 13, 12, 10 |\n| \"at least one in all three\" | centre ≥ 1 | Start centre at 1 when minimising; push it up when maximising |\n\n*Worked pattern:* four groups at 163, 180, 156, 167 with 123 to distribute. Make TT largest with least value: raise all to 180 (uses 54), split 69 as 17 each (uses 68), last 1 to TT → 198. Make badminton smallest with most value: raise all to 180, then 16 each (17 would leave too few to keep others ahead), remaining 5 can't go to badminton without breaking order → 196.\n\n**Consulting-firms style trap**\n30 selected by all four, 0 by exactly three, \"50 by each pair\" → only-two cell = 50 − 30 − 0 = 20. Total in at least one = 600 = 80% of total → total 750 → rejected by all = 150.\n\n**Checks before answering**\n- Did I subtract the \"none\" group before equating the diagram to the total?\n- Did I treat \"and\" as \"only\"?\n- In max/min: is the group I was asked about *strictly* ahead/behind, not tied?\n\n---\n\n## HOW TO USE THIS\n\nBefore the class, read watch-points 1, 2 and 5 — the drawing, the word \"only\", and the chocolate idea are the three things he assumes you'll pick up on the fly. After, the word decoder and the max/min table are the revision. Redo the 198 / 196 pattern from memory once; if it comes out, the topic is done."
